WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:05.220
The climax of Avengers: Endgame featured the
death of a legend — but as perfectly heroic

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:08.600
as the moment was, it could have gone dozens
of different ways.

00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:13.660
In a recent conversation with Collider, the
film's editor, Jeff Ford, went in-depth about

00:00:13.670 --> 00:00:18.200
how the scene where Tony Stark sacrifices
himself to snap Thanos and his invading army

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:20.080
out of existence came to be.

00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:24.840
It's a fascinating deep dive into the genesis
of an unforgettable scene — and also an

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:27.720
interesting and humorous look at Downey's
process.

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:33.070
Ford is the longtime editor for Endgame co-directors
Joe and Anthony Russo, and he's made himself

00:00:33.070 --> 00:00:37.960
a Marvel icon with one contribution to the
Infinity Saga-culminating film: it was he

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:42.320
who put Stark's last words into his mouth,
when he suggested that the Armored Avenger's

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:44.380
retort to Thanos' declaration …

00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:47.840
"I am inevitable!"

00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:51.600
… should mirror the statement he made to
the world at the conclusion of the inaugural

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:54.080
entry into the Marvel Cinematic Universe:

00:00:54.240 --> 00:01:03.220
"And I... am Iron Man."

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:08.280
It was perhaps the single greatest moment
in the MCU up to this point, but according

00:01:08.290 --> 00:01:13.280
to Ford, nobody was exactly sure how it should
play out when the time came to actually shoot

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:14.040
the scene.

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:18.780
Since they were working with a gifted actor
and brilliant improviser in Downey, the filmmakers

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:22.420
basically decided to let him off the chain
and see what would happen.

00:01:22.420 --> 00:01:23.880
Ford told Collider:

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:28.050
"When we were putting together the end of
the movie, when we shot Tony's last moment...

00:01:28.050 --> 00:01:29.920
we shot a bunch of different options.

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:33.030
Robert had different ideas...We give him space
to do that.

00:01:33.030 --> 00:01:35.000
Joe and Anthony are great about improv.

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:38.040
We shot a run of different performances for
that last moment.

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:39.520
Some of them were crazy.

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:41.260
Some of them we would never have used."

00:01:41.260 --> 00:01:46.040
Of course, like everyone else, the interviewer
wanted to know more about those crazy, left-field

00:01:46.040 --> 00:01:50.840
takes that never could have made it into the
movie — but Ford declined to get too specific,

00:01:50.840 --> 00:01:55.680
citing a desire not to, quote, "peel back
the curtain" on Downey's process too much.

00:01:55.680 --> 00:02:00.180
But what he did have to say sounds on brand
for RDJ, and of course, it makes us love the

00:02:00.190 --> 00:02:01.430
actor even more.

00:02:01.430 --> 00:02:02.430
Ford dished:

00:02:02.430 --> 00:02:03.960
"Some of them were jokes.

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:05.560
Some of them were obscenities.

00:02:05.560 --> 00:02:09.700
Some of them were completely emotional, raw,
insane things that he was doing.

00:02:09.700 --> 00:02:12.820
And then some of them were combinations of
all three of those things."

00:02:12.820 --> 00:02:16.920
That is so easy to picture from Downey that
we're actually perfectly content to let our

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:18.520
imaginations run wild.

00:02:18.520 --> 00:02:22.360
We're sure some of these takes were even crazy
enough to have worked, but we're glad that

00:02:22.361 --> 00:02:26.480
we got the iconic moment that we did — and
it was all thanks to the filmmakers wanting

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:31.100
a sense of "symmetry," paralleling Stark's
line with Thanos' "I am inevitable" line.

00:02:31.100 --> 00:02:35.060
Interestingly, the only other version of the
scene that the filmmakers liked nearly as

00:02:35.060 --> 00:02:39.380
much as the final one didn't involve any alternate
versions of the dialogue.

00:02:39.380 --> 00:02:41.600
It actually featured no dialogue at all.

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:42.600
Ford explained:

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:45.880
"In [one] version, Thanos [and Stark] didn't
say anything...

00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:47.240
[Thanos] had the gauntlet.

00:02:47.240 --> 00:02:48.940
He looked at him like, 'I got you.'

00:02:48.940 --> 00:02:49.580
Snapped.

00:02:49.580 --> 00:02:50.280
Looked.

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:51.200
Couldn't believe it.

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:52.080
Turned to Tony.

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:53.660
Tony raised his hand and snapped.

00:02:53.740 --> 00:02:54.820
It was beautiful.

00:02:54.820 --> 00:02:56.880
It worked really, really well."

00:02:56.890 --> 00:03:01.030
The filmmakers realized, however, that they
had an opportunity to cement the themes of

00:03:01.030 --> 00:03:05.660
their film and the arcs of the two characters
involved in the climactic showdown when they

00:03:05.660 --> 00:03:07.390
landed on the final exchange.

00:03:07.390 --> 00:03:08.220
Ford said:

00:03:08.220 --> 00:03:11.260
"What we found... was Thanos needed an arc
in Endgame.

00:03:11.260 --> 00:03:13.520
That arc was his sense of inevitability.

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:17.740
The story we'd been telling was that Thanos'
pitch in that movie is 'no matter how many

00:03:17.750 --> 00:03:22.080
times you try and stop me, you can travel
in time, you can do all these things, you're

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:23.480
never going to win.'

00:03:23.480 --> 00:03:27.440
It's a sense of destiny, of 'I will always
be the one who wins.'"

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:31.960
Ford went on to say that by having Thanos
invoke that sense of destiny at the crucial

00:03:31.960 --> 00:03:36.831
moment only to meet his defeat, the filmmakers
drove home the true theme of Endgame: that

00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:39.320
destiny will always land on the side of right.

00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:40.780
"This is the fight of our lives.

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:47.020
And we're gonna win — whatever it takes."

00:03:48.100 --> 00:03:52.660
It's interesting to think about how many ways
the climax of Endgame could have played out,

00:03:52.670 --> 00:03:56.200
and if we had gotten a different version,
it's just possible that we would be hailing

00:03:56.200 --> 00:03:59.580
that choice as the only one the filmmakers
could possibly have made.

00:03:59.580 --> 00:04:04.200
But we think pretty much every Marvel fan
agrees that the scene we got was utterly perfect

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:08.290
— due in no small part to the fact that
it's tough to imagine a more fitting parting

00:04:08.290 --> 00:04:13.700
shot for an actor who completely embodied
his character for nine films and 11 years.

